                {
                    "file": "common/src/host_power.cpp",
                    "line": 29,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jagpal S Gill",
                        "email": "paligill@gmail.com",
                        "username": "jagpalgill"
                    },
                    "message": "Where is the handler for this state change? The constructor should take an in_arg handler as well.\n\nIs there some way to test this matcher invocation, from what i see you haven't tested it since you don't have a handler?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "common/src/host_power.cpp",
                    "line": 29,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "```hostStateChangedMatch```\nis public and you can call ```.next<...>()``` on it to async block on the signals.\n\n> Is there some way to test this matcher invocation, from what i see you haven't tested it since you don't have a handler?\n\nI did not write a unit test for ```sdbusplus::async::match``` since i assume it's implementation should already be tested in ```sdbusplus```.\n\nI added this matcher since it was requested for eeprom device code updater if i remember correctly. I do not have a use-case for it currently in BIOS code updater.\n\nSo it has not been tested by me. If there are any problems with it we can go back to it, but from my understanding, if we construct it with correct interface and object path, it should just work."
                },
                {
                    "file": "common/src/host_power.cpp",
                    "line": 36,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jagpal S Gill",
                        "email": "paligill@gmail.com",
                        "username": "jagpalgill"
                    },
                    "message": "Shouldn't this be `||` instead of `&&` ? How can state have 2 values at the same time?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "common/src/host_power.cpp",
                    "line": 36,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "```\nstate != stateOn && state != stateOff\n<=>\n! (state == stateOn || state == stateOff)\n```\n\nthe possible values of HostState are\n```\n      enum class HostState\n      {   \n          Off,\n          TransitioningToOff,\n          Standby,\n          Running,\n          TransitioningToRunning,\n          Quiesced,\n          DiagnosticMode,\n      };  \n```\n\nso with this statement i am trying to prevent a caller from passing anything besides on/off into this function."
                },
                {
                    "file": "common/src/host_power.cpp",
                    "line": 38,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jagpal S Gill",
                        "email": "paligill@gmail.com",
                        "username": "jagpalgill"
                    },
                    "message": "Lets log an error message here mentioning invalid in state."
                },
                {
                    "file": "common/src/host_power.cpp",
                    "line": 38,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
